\begin{document}

\begin{frontmatter}
\title{On lexicographic Gr\"obner bases of radical ideals in dimension zero:
interpolation and structure}

\author{X.~Dahan\fnref{moi}}

\fntext[moi]{Supported by the GCOE program ``Math-for-Industry''
of Ky\^ush\^u university}
% Dep\textsuperscript{t} 

\address{Faculty of Mathematics, Ky\^ush\^u university, Fukuoka Japan}

\ead{dahan@math.kyushu-u.ac.jp}

\begin{abstract}
Due to the elimination property held by the lexicographic
monomial order, the corresponding Gr\"obner bases display
strong structural properties from which meaningful informations
can easily be extracted.
We study these properties for radical ideals of (co)dimension zero.
The proof presented relies on a combinatorial decomposition
of the finite set of points whereby  iterated Lagrange interpolation formulas
permit to reconstruct a minimal Gr\"obner basis.
This is the first fully explicit interpolation formula for polynomials
forming a lexicographic Gr\"obner
basis, from which the structure property can easily be read off.
The inductive nature of the proof also yield
a triangular decomposition algorithm from the Gr\"obner basis.
\end{abstract}

\begin{keyword}
Gr\"obner basis \sep Lexicographic order \sep  Interpolation 
\end{keyword}

\end{frontmatter}

\section{Introduction}
\paragraph{Generalities}
The lexicographic monomial order benefits fully of the {\em elimination
property}, implying a lot of structure  in the polynomials
of such Gr\"obner bases. Concretely, for ``non-generic''
Gr\"obner bases, some common  factors
are repeated among several polynomials, inducing some
redundancies.
``Non-generic'' implies   here
Gr\"obner bases which have more polynomials than
the number of variables.
These redundancies explain why
they are often huge, quite impracticable for substantial
computations (as compared to the degree reverse lexicographic order
in particular).
On the other hand, this structure makes easy the extraction of
meaningful informations. For instance, an early application
was the possibility to solve polynomial
systems~\cite[Method~6.10]{Bu85}~\cite{Ka87}
in the case where the number of solutions is finite
(that is when the generated ideal is of
{\em (co)dimension zero}, as we will rather say hereafter).
The structure is also useful to express
the polynomials
in such Gr\"obner bases with  interpolation formulas,
in function of the solution
points. Such formulas allow to get reasonably sharp
upper bounds on the size of coefficients,
as it was achieved for some special cases of lexicographic Gr\"obner bases in~\cite{DaKaSc12,DaSc04,Da09}.
Another application is the possibility
to decompose such ``non-generic'' Gr\"obner bases into
smaller ones.
This principle fits the realm of {\em triangular decompositions},
and the fact that starting to decompose from a \lexGB .  is easier is due
to Lazard~\cite[Section~5]{La92}.
He sketched two methods to perform this decomposition,
and claimed  correctness resorting to Gianni-Kalkbrener's theorem~\cite{Ka87,Gi87}. 
But with no more details, and it appears not obvious whether this is sufficient. 
Probably not, indeed  a proof becomes easy when resorting to the
stronger Theorem (structure) provided here. More details on this
is found in the paragraph {\em Specialization\dots} below.


\paragraph{Structure theorem}
Let $k$ be a field, $k[\X{n}]$ the polynomial ring with $n$ variables
on which is put the lexicographic monomial order $\precc$ for which
$X_1\precc X_2 \precc \cdots \precc X_n$ and $I\subset k[\X{n}]$
a radical ideal of dimension zero (its associated prime ideals
are all maximal). In this case the degree $d(I)$ of $I$
is the finite integer equal to $\dim_k k[\X{n}]/I$.
\medskip

\begin{tabular}{rl}
\Hyp & 
\begin{minipage}{13cm}
Assume that $k$ is infinite, or else that
$d(I) < | k |$.
Moreover, if $k$ is of finite characteristic,
for each associated prime ideal $\frak{m}$
to $I$, the finite field extension $k\, |\, k[\X{n}]/\frak{m}$ is separable
(this is always the case if $k$ is a finite field).
\end{minipage}
\end{tabular}
\medskip

Let $V\subset \con{k}^n$ be the set of common zeroes of the polynomials
in $I$, with coordinates taken in the algebraic closure $\con{k}$ of $k$.
Because of the separability assumption \Hyp~above, the cardinal of $V$
is equal to $d(I)$. 

Let $\G$ be a lexicographic Gr\"obner basis
of $I$. We assume that it is minimal, that is $\LM(\G)$ is a minimal
monomial basis for the monomial ideal $\l\LM(I)\r$.
But we do not necessarily assume that $\G$ is
reduced. Since the polynomial ring is over a field $k$, it does not
matter to require $\G$ to be  {\em monic}: all the polynomials in $\G$
have a leading coefficient equal to 1. 
For $1\le i \le n-1$, let $R_i:=k[\X{i}]$. Given a polynomial
$f\in k[\X{n}]$, let $\LC_i(f)\in R_i$ be the leading coefficient
of $f \in R_i[\XX{i+1}{n}]$. 
Furthermore,
$\LT_i(f)$ and $\LM_i(f)\in k[\XX{i+1}{n}]$ will denote respectively
the leading term and the leading monomial of $f\in R_i[\XX{i+1}{n}]$
yielding the equality $\LT_i(f)=\LC_i(f) \LM_i(f)$.
\medskip

\noindent {\bf Theorem (Structure).} %% lalel{th:structure}
{\em For all $g\in \G,\ g\notin k[\X{n-1}]$, holds:
$$
g\in \l \LC_1(g) \r \ \ (\iff \LC_1(g) \, |\,  g)\ ,
\quad \forall 2\le t\le n-1,\ \ g\in \l \LC_t(g)\r + I_{t-1}.
$$
$$
\text{Moreover,} \qquad \forall t' > t \qquad \text{holds}
\qquad \LC_{t'}(g)\in \l \LC_t(g)\r +I_{t-1}.
$$ 
}
This structure theorem has a direct application in the context of {\em specialization}
of Gr\"obner bases.  The proof of this theorem is easily reduced
to show the existence of {\em one}  Gr\"obner basis which verifies these properties.
