In comparison with~\cite{Le08,FeRaRo06}, the present article provides a similar combinatorial
decomposition of \S~\ref{sec:decomp}
as the one provided by the ``lex-game'' (see \S~2.3 therein)
but has the additional benefits mentioned in 2.-3. above,
which are {\em necessary} to pursue the works
on complexity and on {\tt lextriangular}.
Moreover, the interpolation part of \S~\ref{sec:interp}
is more explicit, with the possibility to provide a good running-time,
improving upon possibly the algorithm of~\cite{Le08}.


