Concerning the structure theorem,
before this theorem was stated in~\cite{MaMo03}, previous works on the
structure have been considered.
In the easy case of a polynomial ring with two variables, Lazard
has found out that the structure theorem hold for any ideal, not only
radical one~\cite{Laz85}.
\medskip

\noindent{\bf Theorem (D. Lazard).} %% \label{th:Lazard}
{\em Let $J\subset k[X_1,X_2]$ be a zero-dimen\-sional ideal,
and $f_1,\ldots,f_r$ a minimal lexicographic Gr\"obner basis
of $J$ for $X_1\precc_{lex} X_2$. Then:
$$
\LC_1(f_i)\in k[X_1]  \quad \text{divides} \quad \LC_1(f_j) \text{ for all }
\quad i \ge j,$$
$$ \text{and} \quad \LC_1(f_i)\quad \text{divides}
\quad \quad f_i \quad \text{as well.}
$$
}
It follows easily a factorization property of the polynomials
in such a Gr\"obner basis,
which is actually the original statement\footnote{However, the formulation above
is more compact and handy, and is equivalent
assuming that we are considering only minimal and monic Gr\"obner bases.}
of Lazard~\cite[Theorem~1~(i)]{Laz85}. In the case of a radical
ideal this is equivalent to Theorem (Structure) aforementioned. Note that
if $I$ is not radical, Theorem~(Structure) does not hold for $n>2$.

Then Gianni and Kalkbrener~\cite{Gi87,Ka87} independently presented a
form of structure theorem stated in the context
of specialization of Gr\"obner bases.

