\paragraph{Application to stability
of Gr\"obner bases under specialization}
The stability of a Gr\"obner basis under a homomorphism map
goes beyond the scope of this article (see~\cite{BaGaSt93} for details).
When the homomorphism map $\phi$ is
a {\em specialization} and takes the following form:  for $1\le \ell
\le n-1$ and $(\alpha_1,\ldots,\alpha_\ell)\in \con{k}^\ell$,
\begin{eqnarray*}
\phi:\con{k}[\X{n}] & \longrightarrow  & \con{k}[\XX{\ell+1}{n}]\\
\ P(\X{n}) & \longmapsto & P(\alpha_1,\ldots,\alpha_\ell,\XX{\ell+1}{n}),
\end{eqnarray*}
then given a monomial order $\precc_\ell$ that eliminates the
variables $\X{\ell}$,
$I\subset k[\X{n}]$ is said to be {\em stable} under $\phi$
if and only if: $\phi(\LT_\ell(I)) = \LT(\phi(I))$ (only the inclusion
$\supset$ is not automatically satisfied).
Hence, if $G$ is a Gr\"obner basis of $I$ this implies that $\phi(G)$
is a Gr\"obner basis of $\phi(I)$. It may happen though that $\phi(G)$
is a Gr\"obner basis of $\l \phi(G)\r \subset
\con{k}[\XX{\ell+1}{n}]$ without verifying $\l \phi(G)\r=\phi(I)$ \cite[Rmk~2,\ Ex.~3]{Gi87}.
If the monomial order is lexicographic and $I$ is radical of dimension
zero as in Hypothesis~(H), then Theorem~(Structure) applies and
implies stability. More precisely:
\begin{Cor}\label{cor:stab}
With the notations and assumptions above,
given a minimal G\"obner basis $\G$ of $I$, and $g\in \G$,
the following equivalence holds:
$$
\phi(\LT_\ell(g)) = 0 \iff \phi(g) =0.
$$
In particular, $\LT(\phi(g))=\phi(\LT_\ell(g))$ and the stability
property holds. Hence $\phi(\G)$ is a Gr\"obner basis of $\phi(I)$.
\end{Cor}
It is noteworthy that Becker in~\cite{Be94} proves that $\phi(G)$
remains a Gr\"obner basis but he does not prove stability\footnote{his proof can not be
adapted. In the proof of the crucial Lemma~1, in Equality~(4) is
assumed a degree decrease that
prevents to consider stability.},
letting
unproved the fact that $\l \phi(G)\r = \phi(I)$.

When all the variables but the largest are specialized, that is when
$\ell=n-1$, Gianni-Kalkbrener~\cite{Gi87,Ka87} has proved that in the
case
of a radical ideal $I$,  the stability property holds. It strongly
relies
on Lemma~5.6 of~\cite{GTZ88}.
 As shows
Corollary~\ref{cor:stab}, Theorem~(Structure) is the genuine
generalization of Gianni-Kalkbrener (which is not the result of
Becker~\cite{Be94}).

\paragraph{Organization of the paper}
In \S~\ref{sec:3} hereunder we treat the case $n=3$ to keep up a geometric
intuition. The other sections \S~\ref{sec:decomp}-\ref{sec:interp}-\ref{sec:conclu}
aim at generalizing to more than 3 variables.
\S~\ref{sec:decomp} introduces the combinatorial decomposition
of the set of points that determines the leading terms of the
minimal Gr\"obner basis. \S~\ref{sec:interp}-\ref{sec:conclu}
are proving this fact by constructing explicitly
polynomials by interpolation (\S~\ref{sec:interp}),
which are proved in \S~\ref{sec:conclu}  to form a Gr\"obner basis.


\section{Warming-up: case of three variables\label{sec:3}}
The first case for which difficulties arise is when $n=3$.
The case $n=2$ is too special to reveal any genuine technical
problem.
Though, the way to combine a decomposition of the zero set
and Lagrange interpolation formula appears
in~\cite[\S~2.2]{Da09}\footnote{as a matter of facts, the use of the {\em equiprojectable decomposition}
becomes obsolete for $n>2$} which we are trying to generalize here to the case of three variables.

\subsection{Set-up\label{ssec:setup}}
The notations hereunder are not restricted to the case $n=3$ and will
be used all along the paper.
\paragraph{Notations} Given an $n$-uplet $y=(y_1,\ldots,y_n)$ and an
$1\le \ell \le n-1$ let $\pi_\ell$ be the projection that forgets the
last $n-\ell+1$ coordinates: $\pi_\ell(y)=(y_1,\ldots , y_\ell)$.
And let $p_\ell(y)=y_\ell$ be the $\ell$-th coordinate function.
Given another integer $\ell'$ such that $\ell < \ell'\le n$,
define $p_{\ell,\ell'}(y):=(y_\ell,\ldots,y_{\ell'})$.
Fibers of projection maps are used intensively and it is convenient to precise
the length of the starting sequence: $\pi_{\ell',\ell}$ denotes the projection
$\pi_{\ell',\ell}(y_1,\ldots,y_\ell')=(y_1,\ldots,y_\ell)$,
The point of  this notation is when taking reciprocal images $\pi_{\ell',\ell}^{-1}(\, \cdot\, )$
to know the dimension of the starting space.

Given a set $S\subset\con{k}^\ell$, and $\alpha\in \con{k}^{\ell-1}$ for
we introduce a convenient notation $S[\alpha]$:
\begin{eqnarray}
\nonumber S:\con{k}^{\ell-1} & \longrightarrow & {\cal
  P}(\con{k}^\ell)
\qquad (set~ of~ parts~ of~ \con{k}^\ell)\\
\label{eq:S[a]}\alpha & \longmapsto & S[\alpha]:=\pi_{\ell,\ell-1}^{-1}(\{\alpha\}) \cap S
\end{eqnarray}
%% Furthermore, $d_S[\alpha]$ will denote $|S[\alpha]|$ and
%% $d_S:=\max_{\alpha \in \con{k}^\ell} d_\alpha$ (bounded since $S$ is finite).

The proofs in this paper deal a lot with minimal bases of monomial ideals
for a which a special notation is necessary:
%% Leaving the ``traditional'' notation  of ideals of leading
%% terms, the notation $\LM(I)$ will refer to:
\begin{Def}\label{def:min(I)}
Given an ideal $I\subset k[\XX{1}{n}]$, $\min(I)$ will denote
the minimal basis of the monomial ideal $\l \LM(I)\r$.
\end{Def} 
If $\G$ is a minimal Gr\"obner basis of $I$, then $\{ \LM(g) \ \st\
g\in \G\}$.

\paragraph{Case n=2}
First, let us briefly review how the case $W\subset \con{k}^n$ Zariski-closed
over $k$, works for $n=2$. The ideal $I(W)$ of vanishing polynomials on $W$
is denoted $J$, and the goal is to determine the minimal basis $\min(J)$
of $\l \LM(J)\r$.
\begin{itemize}
\item $X_1^a$ is in the minimal basis, where $a:=|\pi_1(W)|$.
\item $X_1^c X_2^d$ is in the monomial basis if and only if
$W^d:=\{ y=(y_1,y_2) \in W\ \st\ |\pi_1^{-1}(y_1) \cap W|=d \}$
is not empty, {\em and} $|\pi_1(\cup_{\ell > d}W^{\ell})|=c$.
\end{itemize}
The proof in~\cite{Da09} builds explicitly a Gr\"obner basis from $W$

