to prove these assertions. Let us carry through the case $n=3$.

\paragraph{Strategy}
Let $V\subset \con{k}^3$, Zariski-closed over $k$.
% (or equivalently
% {\em definable} over $k$).
The ideal $I(V)$ of vanishing polynomials
on $V$ is denoted $I$. The strategy can be summarized as follows:
\begin{enumerate}
\item to each couple of integers $(i_2,i_3)\in \N^2$, associate
``naturally'' a subset $\wt{V}^{i_3,i_2}$ that may be empty. If it is not,
this determines a third integer $i_1$ and $\XXX{i}\in \min(I)$.
\item construct by interpolation a polynomial that vanishes on $V$
and that has $\XXX{i}$ for leading monomial. 
\item prove that $\min(I)$ is equal to $\min(I \cap k[X_1,X_2])$
union the $\XXX{i}$ for each 3-uplets $(i_1,i_2,i_3)$  mentioned in 1.
\end{enumerate}
As it stands in this paper, the constructed Gr\"obner basis is not reduced.
Experimentally, it has however smaller coefficients (proved in the
case of 2 variables in~\cite{Da09}). %% TO DO OR NOT ? 

\subsection{Decomposition\label{ssec:dec3}}
Let $V^\ell := \{ y \in V\ \st \ |\pi_{3,2}^{-1}(y_1,y_2) \cap V|
=\ell\}=\{ y \in V\ \st |V[\alpha]|=\ell\}$. It is clear that it defines a partition of $V$ and since $V$
is finite, that almost all $V^\ell$ are empty. Let $i_3$
be such that $V^{i_3}\not=\emptyset$. The shortcut notations $V^{\le i_3}:=\cup_{\ell \le
  i_3} V^\ell$ and $V^{>i_3}=\cup_{\ell > i_3} V^\ell$ will be convenient. Let
$S'_2:=\pi_2(V^{>i_3})$
and $S_2:=\pi_2(V^{\le i_3})$.
\begin{Lem}
$\pi_2(V)= S_2 \cup S_2'$ and $S_2 \cap S'_2=\emptyset$.
\end{Lem}
\begin{pf}
Given $(y_1,y_2)\in S_2$, there exists $y_3\in \con{k}$
such that $(y_1,y_2,y_3)\in V^{\le i_3}$. Thus
$|\pi_{3,2}^{-1}(y_1,y_2) \cap V|\le i_3$,
whereas if $(x_1,x_2)\in S_2'$, then
$|\pi_{3,2}^{-1}(x_1,x_2) \cap V| > i_3$.
This shows that $S_2\cap S_2'=\emptyset$.
Since $V=V^{>i_3} \cup V^{\le i_3}$, holds $\pi_2(V)=S_2 \cup S_2'$.
\end{pf}
Note that $S_2$ and $S_2'$ depends on $i_3$.
For the next step, let us introduce for $\ell \in \N$:
\begin{eqnarray}
\label{eq:decV3}V^{i_3,\ell}&:=&\{ y=(y_1,y_2,y_3)\in V\  \st \ |\pi_{2,1}^{-1}( y_1 ) \cap S_2 | = \ell \}\\
\wt{V}^{i_3,\ell}& :=& \{ y=(y_1,y_2,y_3)\in V^{i_3}\  \st \ | \pi_{2,1}^{-1}( y_1 ) \cap S_2 | = \ell \}=V^{i_3,\ell}\cap
V^{i_3}.\label{eq:decWTV3}
\end{eqnarray}
Again it is clear that $(V^{i_3,\ell})_{\ell\in \N}$ is a partition of
$V$, and that almost all $V^{i_3,\ell}$ are empty.
Thus, $(\wt{V}^{i_3,\ell})_{\ell\in \N}$ is a partition of $V^{i_3}$
and almost all $\wt{V}^{i_3,\ell}$ are empty.
Let $i_2$
be such that $\wt{V}^{i_3,i_2}$ is not empty, and let $V^{i_3,\le
  i_2}:=\cup_{\ell \le i_2} V^{i_3,\ell}$ while
$V^{i_3,>i_2}:=\cup_{\ell >i_2} V^{i_3,\ell}$. Finally let
$S_1':=\pi_1(V^{i_3,>i_2})$
and $S_1:=\pi_1(V^{i_3,\le i_2})$ and define $i_1:=|S_1'|$.
\begin{Lem}
$\pi_1(V)=S_1 \cup S_1'$ and $S_1\cap S_1'=\emptyset$
\end{Lem}
\begin{pf}
The first equality directly results from the fact that
$V=V^{i_3,>i_2}\cup V^{i_3,\le i_2}$. As for the second,
if $y_1\in S_1$ then $| \pi_{2,1}^{-1}(y_1) \cap S_2 |\le i_2$
whereas $x_1\in S_1'$ must fulfill
$| \pi_{2,1}^{-1}(y_1) \cap S_2 | >  i_2$.
\end{pf}
The insight of the decomposition is that
$\XXX{i}$ is in the monomial basis  
$\min(I)$ of $\l \LM(I)\r$. Let $\LL(\wt{V})$ and $\LL '(\wtv)$ be the sets:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:L(V)3}
\LL(\wtv):=\{ (i_2 , i_3) \in \N^2 \ \st \ \wtv^{i_3,i_2} \not= \emptyset\}.
\end{equation}
\begin{equation}\label{eq:L'(V)3}
\LL'(\wtv):=\{ (i_1,i_2,i_3) \in \N^3 \st\ (i_2,i_3)\in \LL(\wtv)
\ \text{and}\ i_1=|\pi_1(V^{i_3,>i_2})|=|S_1'| \}.
\end{equation}
%% The proof consists in constructing effectively polynomials
%% with leading monomials equal to $\XXX{i}$ for all such triplets
%% $(i_1,i_2,i_3)$ as dealt with the above, and to show that they form a
%% minimal Gr\"obner basis of $I$. Before going through it in the
%% \S~\ref{sec:interpol3},
Let us illustrate how the decomposition works on an oversimplified but
eloquent enough example.

\paragraph{Example} Consider the finite set of points
$V\subset\Z^3\subset\con{\Q}^3$ described hereafter. Define $\pi_1(V):=\{0,1,2,3,4,5\}$,
and $\pi_2(V)$ the points $\bullet$, having integer coordinates
in Figure~\ref{fig:points}.%% inside the region with integer coordinates.
