Proposition~\ref{prop:f3} allows to prove the following:
\begin{Lem}\label{lem:K2}
Let $K:=\l \LM( I ) \r :\l \LM(f)\r$ and $L:=\l \LM(I)\r + \l\LM(f)\r$.
One has: $K = \l \LM(J)\r$ and $L:=\l \LM(J')\r$.

The same equalities remain true when taking $X_3$-elimination ideals:
if  $K_2$ denotes $K\cap k[X_1,X_2]$ and $L_2$ denotes
$L\cap k[X_1,X_2]$, then hold $K_2=\l \LM(J_2)\r$ and $L_2:=\l \LM(J'_2)\r$.
\end{Lem}
\begin{pf}
Treating appart the special case $n=3$ does bring any simplification
in regard to the general proof of Corollaries~\ref{cor:L} and~\ref{cor:K}.
It almost suffices indeed to replace  $n$ by $3$.
These proofs show as a byproduct that the following isomorpshism
of $k$-vector spaces
{\small
\begin{eqnarray}
\nonumber \theta : k[X_1,X_2,X_3]/\l \LM(I)\r  & \longrightarrow & 
k[X_1,X_2,X_3]/L \times k[X_1,X_2,X_3]/ K \\
\label{eq:theta3} m \bmod \l \LM(I)\r & \longmapsto & m \bmod
\LM(f) \  ,\ m \quo \LM(f)
\end{eqnarray}
}
coincide with $\psi$ in~\eqref{eq:psi3}.
\end{pf}
The above map~\eqref{eq:theta3} proves that
$\min(I) = \min(L) \cup \{ m \LM(f) \ |\ m\in \min(K)\}$,
and Lemma~\eqref{lem:K2} that $\min(L)=\min(J)$, $\min(K)=\min(J')$,
$\min(J_{n-2}) = \min(L_{n-2})$ and $\min(K_{n-2})=\min(J_{n-2}')$.
Thus, $min(I) = \min(J') \cup \{ m \LM(f) \ |\ m\in \min(J)\}$
which is the point 1.

Moreover, the isomorphism $\theta$ in~\eqref{eq:theta} allows
to deduce without efforts the following one:
{\small
\begin{eqnarray*}
\nonumber \theta : k[X_1,X_2]/\l \LM(I_2)\r  & \longrightarrow & 
k[X_1,X_2]/L_2 \times k[X_1,X_2]/ K_2 \\
 m \bmod \l \LM(I_2)\r & \longmapsto & m \bmod
\LM(f) \  ,\ m \quo \LM(f)
\end{eqnarray*}
}
Allowing to prove that  $\min(I_{n-2}) = \min(L_2)\setminus \{ \LM(f)\}
\cup \{ \LM(f) m \ |\ m\in \min(K_2)\}$ and with Lemma~\ref{lem:K2}
that $\min(I_{n-2}) = \min(J_2')\setminus \{ \LM(f)\}
\cup \{ \LM(f) m \ |\ m\in \min(J_2)\}$. 
This is the point 2. aforementioned, and according to the points 1.-6.
this achieves the proof of Theorem~\ref{th:LM3}.

\section{Combinatorial decomposition of a set of points\label{sec:decomp}}
The generalization to $n>3$ coordinates of the decomposition
sketched in~\ref{ssec:dec3} is carried over in this section.
Let $V\subset \con{k}^n$ be the set of common zeroes of the
polynomials in $I$.

\subsection{Definitions}
There is actually not one decomposition, but one associated to each
multi-integer $\ii=(i_2 , \ldots , i_n)\in \N^{n-1}$, which we {\bf fix}
from now on and within this section. The inductive definition requires
some notations.

Given $S$ a finite subset of $\con{k}^\ell$ define:
\begin{eqnarray*}
\phi_{S,\ell}:V & \longrightarrow & \N\\
 y & \longmapsto & | \pi_{\ell,\ell-1}^{-1} (\pi_{\ell-1}(y)) \cap
 S|.
\end{eqnarray*}
