\subsection{Application to the settings of~\S~\ref{sec:decomp}}
Of course, the above construction is tailored to be used with
the two sequences $(S_1,\ldots,S_{n-1})$ and $(S_1',\ldots,S_{n-1}')$
of sets introduced in the previous section.
Recall that  the algebras $A_t$ are  equal
to $\con{k}[\XX{t+1}{n}]$ for $t=1,\ldots,n-1$.

%% Recall that $S_j:=\pi_j(V^{i_1,\ldots,i_{j-1},\le i_{j}})$, and that $V^{i_1,\ldots,i_{j-1},>i_{j}} \cap  V^{i_1,\ldots,i_{j-1},\le i_j} = \emptyset$.
%% So as by defining $S'_j:= \pi_j(V^{i_1,\ldots,i_{j-1},> i_j})$, yields $S_j' \cap S_j = \emptyset$ (PROOF?).


\begin{Def}\label{def:gi}
 $\ii:=(i_2,\ldots,i_n)\in \N^{n-1}$ be such that
for all $j=n,\ldots,2$,
$\wtv^{i_n,\ldots,i_3,i_2}\not = \emptyset$.
And define $\SS:=(S_1,\ldots,S_{n-1})$,
$\SS':=(S_1',\ldots,S_{n-1}')$ obtained
from the decomposition of $V$ and attached to $\ii$ of \S~\ref{sec:decomp}.
Let 
$$
{\cal S}_{n-1}:=\{ (a_1,\ldots,a_{n-1}) \ : \ a_1 \in S_1, \ (a_1,a_2)\in S_2,
\ldots , \ (a_1,\ldots,a_{n-1}) \in S_{n-1}\},
$$
and $f_V:{\cal S}_{n-1}\to \con{k}[X_n]$, $\alpha \mapsto 
X_n^{i_n - |V[\alpha]|} \prod_{\beta \in V
[\alpha]} X_n - \beta_n$. With these notations, define the polynomial:
$$
g_\ii:= L_{\SS,\SS'}( f_V )(\XX{1}{n-1})\in \con{k}[\XX{1}{n}]
%% \quad (TO DO:~over~k)
$$ 
\end{Def}

As done for the case $n=3$, these are candidates for
a minimal Gr\"obner basis of $I$.
Before proving this in \S~\ref{sec:conclu}
let us show that these polynomials verify the 
conclusions of Theorem~(Structure) stated in the introduction.


\begin{Cor}
The leading monomial of $g_\ii$ 
is $\Xab{1}{n}{i}$.
\end{Cor}
\begin{pf}
It suffices to show that for any $\alpha\in {\cal S}_{n-1}$,
$\LM(f_V(\alpha))=X_n^{i_n}$ and to apply Corollary~\ref{cor:LM}.
The former is clear from the definition of $f_V$.
\end{pf}

Next, let us adapt the construction  of Lemma~\ref{lem:interp}
to $g_\ii$. Fix  $1\le t \le n-1$ and take $\alpha\in {\cal S}_{t}$.
Define $\TT^\alpha=(T_1^\alpha,\ldots,T_{n-t}^\alpha)$
and $\TT'^\alpha=(T_1'^\alpha,\ldots,T_{n-t}'^\alpha)$ as below
($p_{ij}$ is defined in the beginning of the
\S~\ref{ssec:setup}):
$$
T_1^\alpha:=p_{t+1}(S_{t+1}[\alpha])\
, \
T_2^\alpha:=p_{t+2,t+1}(\pi_{t+2,t}^{-1}(\alpha)\cap S_{t+2}),
\ldots,
T_{n-t}^\alpha:=p_{n,t+1}(\pi_{n,t+1}^{-1}(\alpha) \cap S_{n-1}).
$$
And similarly  using $S'_j$ rather than $S_j$

